-- check.cub — the type checker as a term: Remark 5.26 “no outer layer” (evaluator, type checker and
-- optimizer are terms of the same language),
-- in §5.5 “What the length counts” of the earlier paper "Respecting Equality", cited in this repository before 2026-11-11 (docs/index.html of the
-- earlier repository avikj/math, last changed 8 Oct 2026, sha256 984fafe88eb6…; no copy is in this repository;
-- its §5 “Complete inference is deterministic and step-optimal” is restated by number in EXPOSITION.md, removed at f00bd0f, readable as git show adaebcd:EXPOSITION.md).
-- The counterpart in the repository's copy of the paper (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf,
-- 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…, §1.2 “Programs about programs” and
-- §6.2 “Specialization as a program over the language’s own definitions”):
-- "The checker is itself a term: it analyzes the evaluator's values through a structured observation
-- %view". Its input is the code of a term in which
-- every node carries the term's value: the machine computed it from the compiled subterm, once,
-- with each node's value built from its parts' values. A binder's code is a function: a term
-- binder takes a variable's value and type, a dimension binder a dimension, and gives the code
-- of the body; instantiating a binder is applying it. A variable's node carries its value and
-- type, so there is no context: a fresh variable is (%var n T), a fresh dimension (%dim n) (n its
-- source name, a word), each
-- made once where it is introduced. A system's branches are thunks of their codes; under a
-- clause the checker restricts the thunk and forces it, so a branch is computed under its face.
-- The checker reads values through %view, which gives a value's head as data whose fields are
-- the value's parts: application, projection, composition, Glue, transport and the endpoints of
-- a path variable are the machine's own steps. Checked definitions, their types and the data
-- types are reached through the tables %gval, %gty, %dtab and %dent (words index them).
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- codes
data Tm
  = tv x T
  | ti i
  | tg n V
  | tlam nm b V
  | tapp f a V
  | tpair a b V
  | tfst p V
  | tsnd p V
  | tpi A nm b V
  | tsigma A nm b V
  | tuniv l V
  | tpathp A a b V
  | tplam nm b V
  | tpapp p r V
  | tc0
  | tc1
  | tneg r V
  | tmeet r s V
  | tjoin r s V
  | teq0 r V
  | teq1 r V
  | tfand a b V
  | tfor a b V
  | ttt
  | tff
  | tforall nm b V
  | tsys bs V
  | tcomp A u a V
  | tgluet A S V
  | tglue S a V
  | tunglue S b V
  -- a composition of types (its faces lines of types from A) and the unglue of its elements
  | thcompu A S V
  | tunglueu S b V
  | tlet t nm b V
  | tann t T V
  | tdata d ps V
  | tcon d c fs ds V
  | telim d P B x F V
  | tcase d B x F V
  | thcomp u a V
  | tforce t V
  | tthunk t V
  | tlevel
  | tlzero
  | tlsuc l V
  | tlmax a b V
  | tword
  | tlit V
  | twsucc a V
  | twop k a b V
  | twelim P w z s V
  | tproj d k x V
  | trec d fs V
  -- elaboration: an implicit argument to find (V a fresh variable standing for it); a λ or an
  -- application whose kind (function or path) was guessed (g: #0 λ, #1 line, #2 application,
  -- #3 path application)
  | thole k V
  | tguess k g t V
  -- an application with holes among its arguments
  | tspine t V
  -- guarded recursion: the type of clocks, the type of the ticks of a clock, the closed clock
  | tclock
  | ttick k V
  | tclock0
  | ttick0
